            "title": "jupyter-mcp_read_notebook",
            "raw_output": {
              "output": "```yaml\nkernelspec:\n  display_name: R 4.5.3 (xr)\n  language: R\n  name: xr\nlanguage_info:\n  codemirror_mode: ''\n  file_extension: .R\n  mimetype: text/x-R\n  name: R\n  nbconvert_exporter: ''\n  pygments_lexer: r\n  version: 4.5.3\n```\n\n### Cell 0\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e000\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n# Exercise: How many nodes?\n\nCan a neural net tell **EDM** from **rap**? Do more nodes help?\n\nLet's revisit the Spotify data from the lecture 13 exercise and compare logistic regression, a random forest, and neural nets with 2, 10, and 50 hidden nodes.```\n\n### Cell 1\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e001\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Setup```\n\n### Cell 2\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e002\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nlibrary('tidymodels')\n\noptions(repr.plot.res = 250, repr.plot.height = 3, repr.plot.width = 5)```\n\n### Cell 3\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e003\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## The data\n\nEach row is one song. `genre` is `rap` or `edm`. The predictors are `danceability` and `tempo`.```\n\n### Cell 4\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e004\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nsongs <- read.csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rfordatascience/tidytuesday/main/data/2020/2020-01-21/spotify_songs.csv')\n\nsongs <- songs |>\n    filter(playlist_genre %in% c('edm', 'rap')) |>\n    distinct(track_id, .keep_all = TRUE) |>\n    mutate(genre = factor(playlist_genre, levels = c('rap', 'edm'))) |>\n    select(track_name, track_artist, genre, danceability, tempo)\n\nsongs |> count(genre)```\n\n### Cell 5\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e005\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## Setup for the models\n\nRun these cells. Do not change them.\n\n- Split the songs in half: `train` and `test`.\n- `train_small` has only 100 songs from `train`.\n- `fit_nn()` fits a neural net with `h` hidden nodes. It normalizes the predictors first.\n- `accuracy_of()` finds the share of songs a model gets right.```\n\n### Cell 6\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e006\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nset.seed(512)\nsplit <- initial_split(songs, prop = 0.5, strata = genre)\ntrain <- training(split)\ntest  <- testing(split)\n\nset.seed(3)\ntrain_small <- train |> slice_sample(n = 100)```\n\n### Cell 7\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e007\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\nrec <- recipe(genre ~ danceability + tempo, data = songs) |>\n    step_normalize(all_predictors())\n\nfit_nn <- function(h, data) {\n    set.seed(1)\n    workflow() |>\n        add_recipe(rec) |>\n        add_model(\n            mlp(hidden_units = h, epochs = 1000, penalty = 0) |>\n                set_mode('classification') |>\n                set_engine('nnet', MaxNWts = 5000)\n        ) |>\n        fit(data = data)\n}\n\naccuracy_of <- function(fit, data) {\n    pred <- predict(fit, new_data = data, type = 'class')$.pred_class\n    mean(pred == data$genre)\n}```\n\n### Cell 8\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e008\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 1. Small data: 100 songs\n\n`compare_models()` fits five models: logistic regression, a random forest, and neural nets with 2, 10, and 50 hidden nodes. It returns two accuracies for each model:\n\n- `train_accuracy`: on the songs used to fit the model.\n- `test_accuracy`: on the `test` songs, which the model has not seen.\n\nRun the cell. It uses only `train_small`, which has 100 songs.```\n\n### Cell 9\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e009\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\ncompare_models <- function(train_data) {\n    set.seed(512)\n    fits <- list(\n        logistic = logistic_reg() |> fit(genre ~ danceability + tempo, data = train_data),\n        forest   = rand_forest(trees = 200) |> set_mode('classification') |> set_engine('ranger') |>\n                       fit(genre ~ danceability + tempo, data = train_data),\n        `nn 2`   = fit_nn(2, train_data),\n        `nn 10`  = fit_nn(10, train_data),\n        `nn 50`  = fit_nn(50, train_data)\n    )\n\n    tibble(\n        model = names(fits),\n        train_accuracy = map_dbl(fits, accuracy_of, data = train_data),\n        test_accuracy  = map_dbl(fits, accuracy_of, data = test)\n    )\n}\n\nresults_small = compare_models(train_small)\n\nresults_small```\n\n### Cell 10\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e010\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Check:** `nn 50` should be right for about 0.99 of the training songs.\n\n- Which model is best on the **training** songs? Which is best on the **test** songs?\n- What happens to the test accuracy of the nets as the number of nodes goes up?\n- A net with 50 hidden nodes learns 201 numbers. How many songs did we give it?```\n\n### Cell 11\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: c64a59cb-cc6a-46a6-bd9c-96442aa2a7cf\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 2. Chart the output\n\nPick a visualization for the output and make the chart below. Write the code by hand or prompt it with `OpenCode`.```\n\n### Cell 12\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: ee43928c-a5dc-48a7-80bc-29071cdf87a9\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n# Chart code goes here\n\n```\n\n### Cell 13\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e011\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 3. More data\n\nRun `compare_models()` again, with all of `train` (about 5,500 songs).```\n\n### Cell 14\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e012\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n```\n\n### Cell 15\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e013\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n**Check:** the nets should now be close to the forest, at about 0.82 on the test songs.\n\n- Is `nn 50` still much worse than `nn 2`? Do more nodes help now?\n- Logistic regression is stuck at about 0.60. Why? (Think about the stripe in the lecture 13 exercise.)```\n\n### Cell 16\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 19906aa5-45de-4b01-986a-363eeb1a0f63\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 4. Plot the results when using all the data```\n\n### Cell 17\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: 96b5d031-0507-49e3-9cd4-8e5db5043259\ntype: code\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```python\n# Chart code goes here\n\n```\n\n### Cell 18\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e014\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n## 5. Which model?\n\nYou must pick one model for a new playlist with **100 labeled songs**. Which do you pick?```\n\n### Cell 19\n\n#### Metadata\n\n```yaml\nid: e015\ntype: markdown\n```\n\n#### Source\n\n```\n*Your answer:*```",
